 declared Yui Audit A, self-containment, and format rules read for this task. I treated H001, H002, M001, and M002 as the only conditional mathematical inputs; I did not inspect or rely on source proofs, other inference bodies, old reviews, or external task material. This review does not certify carrier identity, canonical hash binding, transmitted-material readback, or source fidelity. The first unsupported implication is in S001: q>dim_H(E) implies H^q(E)=0. The only dimension theorem input M001 supplies the opposite-side implication d<dim_H(A) => H^d(A)=∞. The asserted threshold implication is neither a direct input nor proved locally. Acceptance stops there; later gaps are diagnostic only.\",\"checks\":{\"inputs\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The listed graph inputs are H001 (E nonempty), H002 (E compact), M001 (the stated lower-dimension threshold implication with Borel extended-metric and NNReal hypotheses), and M002 (the stated countable-sum integral identity with countability and AEMeasurability hypotheses). They are the only premises credited in this packet-only review. This is not source-fidelity verification.\",\"locations\":[\"/inputs/0\",\"/inputs/1\",\"/inputs/2\",\"/inputs/3\",\"/inference/inputs\"]},\"argument\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"First unsupported implication: S001 asserts the Hausdorff-dimension threshold implication q>dim_H(E) => H^q(E)=0, without an input for it or a local proof. M001 states only d<dim_H(A) => H^d(A)=∞. Consequently the covering-cost argument's starting point is unsupported. Independently, S003 later invokes the general Lipschitz-image dimension inequality and the theorem that [0,1] has Hausdorff dimension 1; neither is among the inputs nor proved locally. Other standard measure/content steps in S002 are also asserted rather than fully derived, but they occur after the first gap.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/inputs/2/statement\",\"/inference/argument/2/text\"]},\"dependency_accounting\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The actual prose uses substantive unlisted results. At S001 it uses the upper-side Hausdorff dimension threshold implication. At S003 it uses the Lipschitz image dimension inequality and the nonempty-interior dimension theorem (plus the interval dimension conclusion). A calculation-policy entry permitting specified product-metric calculations cannot import any of these results. M001 and M002 do not supply them.\",\"locations\":[\"/logic/calculations/0\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/inference/argument/2/text\",\"/inference/applications\"]},\"output\":{\"verdict\":\"UNVERIFIED\",\"notes\":\"The prose claims both inequalities and then equality for dim_H(E×I), but its conclusion is not established because the upper-bound argument already has an unsupported premise and the D=0 lower-bound branch imports unproved results. Thus the target statement is identifiable, but derivation of the exact output is not verified.\",\"locations\":[\"/output/statement\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/inference/argument/2/text\"]},\"scope\":{\"verdict\":\"UNVERIFIED\",\"notes\":\"The argument describes branches for n=0, D=0, and D>0 and says it combines the resulting bounds, but the packet does not give a complete formal account of quantifier introduction/discharge for the arbitrary exponents, scales, covers, and error tolerances. In particular, S002's arbitrary cover and epsilon estimates are used to claim a content infimum bound without a complete local derivation. No independent scope defect is needed to establish the earlier failure.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/locals/2\",\"/inference/locals/3\",\"/inference/locals/5\",\"/inference/locals/6\",\"/inference/locals/7\",\"/inference/locals/8\",\"/inference/argument/1/text\"]},\"applicability\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"M001 is bound in S002 to E and an NNReal coercion of s with 0<s<D; its stated Borel extended-metric and NNReal conditions are asserted to hold for Euclidean space but not fully documented beyond that assertion. M002 is bound to the countable index and restricted Lebesgue setup; the AEMeasurability claim is asserted in the application record, but its proof is only the prose assertion that the summands are constants times indicators of open intervals. More importantly, neither M application supplies S001's upper threshold implication or S003's Lipschitz-image/interval-dimension results. No other M/E application is recorded.\",\"locations\":[\"/inputs/2/source_contract\",\"/inputs/3/source_contract\",\"/inference/applications/0\",\"/inference/applications/1\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/inference/argument/2/text\"]},\"statement_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"UNVERIFIED\",\"notes\":\"The packet gives a concise target statement and states the needed product metric in the proof, but the output does not itself spell out the product space/metric convention. More critically for the argument's claimed contents, the precise Hausdorff measure/content conventions used in S001–S002 are introduced in prose rather than supplied as definitions or direct inputs; S002 refers to a pinned formula not included in the packet. These omissions prevent complete independent interpretation of all asserted intermediate claims.\",\"locations\":[\"/output/statement\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/inference/argument/1/text\",\"/logic/primitives\"]},\"object_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The prose contains mathematical objects and operations not fully accounted for by the declarations/inventories. In particular S003 introduces a map p and uses the Lipschitz-image theorem without a local declaration for p; S002 uses restricted Lebesgue measure, indicators, lintegrals, and Hausdorff content formula objects not declared among primitives, definitions, or locals. Several locals are declared but their claimed existence/basis is not proved, e.g. the zero-diameter replacement cover and arbitrary product-cover constructions. An inventory omission alone is not